<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-number-patterns">
  <title>Sequences</title>
  <objectives xml:id="li-number-sequences"><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol>
      <li><p>Describe numerical patterns in words and rules.</p></li>
      <li><p>Continue arithmetic, geometric and other sequences.</p></li>
      <li><p>Create tables and explain limits of predictions based on finite terms.</p></li>
    </ol></objectives>
  <section xml:id="number-patterns-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>A sequence is an ordered list of numbers governed by a rule or relationship. Some patterns increase by a constant difference; others multiply by a constant factor or follow a different relationship.</p></introduction>
    <p>Use tables, diagrams and verbal rules before generalising with symbols. Several early terms may fit more than one possible rule, so a rule must be stated and justified.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="number-patterns-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-number-patterns">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Continue 4, 7, 10, 13 and describe one rule.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>16 and 19 follow by adding 3 each time.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-number-patterns">
      <statement><p>Find the tenth term of the arithmetic sequence 2, 5, 8, 11, …</p></statement>
      <answer><p>2+9×3=29.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
